#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510483} :activity/id #uuid "7eb5815e-ec1b-5309-9ada-af8c6f2e1f20" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049217 :topic/id #uuid "e446a53c-cc70-58f0-b872-8d6e7a9c434e" :topic/math-academy-id 4123} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510483 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510388 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2332428c-0956-5edc-8fe6-022460583d2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{5}\\,{e}^{-4.5\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509763} {:answer/id #uuid "4099daa4-c8e4-54d9-9751-3571d0836455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\,{e}^{5\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509825} {:answer/id #uuid "5e998d67-7741-5d32-adff-5aad49d3a8ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta \\,{e}^{-4.5\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509884} {:answer/id #uuid "7ecdaf55-77a9-5b17-80ef-8da0aeac0d0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{4.5}\\,{e}^{-5\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509963} {:answer/id #uuid "d5d4114d-abec-5620-b7d3-44264b2a1f94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{-5}\\,{e}^{4.5\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510142}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509763} :answer-field/id #uuid "afa1de34-af75-5f8e-b326-c843f5d31eb8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510332}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4a460e59-a60e-5c01-b0d6-ac09a000a8e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-159874" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{5}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼\\text{Exp}(\\theta )$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{5},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{x}_{i}=4.5.\n$$\n\nFind the likelihood function of the sample.\n\n*Hint: The probability density function for an exponential random variable is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x},\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=5$ independent exponential random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x},\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\theta {e}^{-\\theta {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-S\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$ \n\nWe are told that $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=4.5$ and $n=5.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\theta }^{5}\\,{e}^{-4.5\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "123ec72f-798d-521a-b71f-a350724a6d80" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510516}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510506 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510400 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13a5d853-1f1e-50a0-8e00-2fa1064e40e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{3}(28.7)^{-\\theta -1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509721} {:answer/id #uuid "2146d1c2-145c-5e19-be13-24bf25afba4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{4}(28.7)^{-\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509754} {:answer/id #uuid "b2a34928-e7d0-5f70-87a4-51288f7f7d34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{28.7}(3)^{-\\theta -1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510074} {:answer/id #uuid "def5dc94-633d-576b-941d-07e8b9c94d61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{2}(28.7)^{-\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510162} {:answer/id #uuid "eba3b892-df42-5754-afb3-26d55a0f358f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\theta (28.7)^{-\\theta -1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510196}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509721} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0a24af9-2c20-5bcb-882d-da71398e4661" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510320}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "67f027bd-5627-5870-ad62-8c955e4cf931" :question/math-academy-id "q-159919" :question/problem "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}$ be I.I.D. random variables with probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1},\\,x\\ge 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter. Find the likelihood function of the sample $4.1,\\,2.5,\\,2.8.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=3$ independent random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1},\\,x\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\theta {x}_{i}^{-\\theta -1}) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{-\\theta -1} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})^{-\\theta -1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =4.1\\cdot 2.5\\cdot 2.8=28.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=3.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nL(\\theta )={\\theta }^{3}(28.7)^{-\\theta -1}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "581d75e5-f869-52ac-9527-20195bb5d14d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510534}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510516 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510408 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f431101-4c6d-5bf2-ba99-85b859abf503" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{11}\\,{e}^{-8.8\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509854} {:answer/id #uuid "534b0bea-259c-590f-b217-8d6213f5b072" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{8.8}\\,{e}^{-11\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509861} {:answer/id #uuid "7d59090d-226b-567f-b107-8cc219edf98e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{-11}\\,{e}^{8.8\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509959} {:answer/id #uuid "7f080b35-a5ae-5e2b-bd64-31274fadcc37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{11}\\,{e}^{-\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509965} {:answer/id #uuid "cb96d045-9d55-5deb-9924-204c6531a1bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{-8.8}\\,{e}^{11\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510116}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509854} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f28c710-ad63-5f90-ab4c-dca1c9762afd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7231eacb-7b8d-5da7-a852-73cf2a0b08b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-159875" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{11}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼\\text{Exp}(\\theta )$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{11},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{11}{x}_{i}=8.8.\n$$\n\nFind the likelihood function of the sample.\n\n*Hint: The probability density function for an exponential random variable is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x},\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=11$ independent exponential random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x},\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\theta {e}^{-\\theta {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-S\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$ \n\nWe are told that $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=8.8$ and $n=11.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\theta }^{11}\\,{e}^{-8.8\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "758ec95c-458e-58ad-b910-88dba5d83555" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510506}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510526 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510401 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09c1d491-402e-555e-a666-7f7c4e87d6ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4.225-0.35\\theta -2{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509710} {:answer/id #uuid "78a7b057-e13c-59a5-9c17-b9e7479f7400" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8.45+0.7\\theta +4{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509944} {:answer/id #uuid "811cdd6d-776a-59e7-8f58-71af09a1c53a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4.225-0.7\\theta -2{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509970} {:answer/id #uuid "8f42f131-75fe-5641-ab8b-d247827641d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4.225+0.35\\theta +2{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509996} {:answer/id #uuid "ff1fb752-a169-5228-a97e-05992483d175" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8.45-0.7\\theta -4{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510237}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509970} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f8fd110-202a-5667-bf26-f7d318d06cc6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "67f89f72-de60-5983-9ba7-f68316fa9c35" :question/math-academy-id "q-159972" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼N(\\theta ,1),$  where $\\theta $ is an unknown mean.  Given that the likelihood function of the sample $0.3,\\,1.2,\\,-2.6,\\,0.4$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=\\frac{1}{4{\\pi }^{2}}\\,{e}^{\\,g(\\theta )},\n$$\n\nwhat is the expression for $g(\\theta )?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sigma \\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{x-\\theta }{\\sigma })}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-\\sum ({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(\\sum {x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(C-2S\\theta +n{\\theta }^{2})/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $C={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}.$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =0.3+1.2+(-2.6)+0.4 \\\\  & =-0.7 \\\\ C & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(0.3)^{2}+(1.2)^{2}+(-2.6)^{2}+(0.4)^{2} \\\\  & =8.45\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=4.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =(2\\pi )^{-4/2}\\,{e}^{-(8.45-2(-0.7)\\theta +4{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{\\pi }^{2}}\\,{e}^{\\,-4.225\\,-\\,0.7\\theta \\,-\\,2{\\theta }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(\\theta )=-4.225-0.7\\theta -2{\\theta }^{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9adaeb98-58da-5087-9e4e-fb6fed2b0b66"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510534 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510380 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ee6b5b6-79d8-5f3b-a3b5-ea4295831ec4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-29.4+8.98\\theta -2{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509788} {:answer/id #uuid "36bfab97-2213-5db0-bc57-64be646f86f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-29.4+4.49\\theta -2{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509806} {:answer/id #uuid "f0783fcb-15c0-52e8-a012-1b2d6692109d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-14.7+8.98\\theta -2{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510206} {:answer/id #uuid "f801fc62-3fdd-5360-808c-c7be45810737" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8.98+14.7\\theta -2{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510216} {:answer/id #uuid "faf6b123-23f4-5859-a6a3-7c67537a6a69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4.49+29.4\\theta -2{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510226}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510206} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9645430-c3dd-5a0d-a5c2-2b90bb8a3076" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3a232b81-c933-5282-aa6a-6ac51618b1b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-159951" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼N(\\theta ,1),$  where $\\theta $ is an unknown mean. For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{x}_{i}=8.98,\\,{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{x}_{i}^{2}=29.4.\n$$\n\nGiven that the likelihood function of the sample is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=\\frac{1}{4{\\pi }^{2}}\\,{e}^{\\,g(\\theta )},\n$$\n\nwhat is the expression for $g(\\theta )?$\n\n*Hint: The probability density function for a normal random variable with mean $\\theta $ and variance $1$ is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-\\sum ({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(\\sum {x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(C-2S\\theta +n{\\theta }^{2})/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $C={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}.$\n\nWe are told that $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=8.98,$ $C={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}=29.4,$ and $n=4.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =(2\\pi )^{-4/2}\\,{e}^{-(29.4-2(8.98)\\theta +4{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{\\pi }^{2}}\\,{e}^{\\,-14.7\\,+\\,8.98\\theta \\,-\\,2{\\theta }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(\\theta )=-14.7+8.98\\theta -2{\\theta }^{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ae0bb4af-619e-5c31-bd50-96b13e3c5a74" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510526}}] :activity/title "Likelihood Functions for Continuous Probability Distributions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509685}]}
